  "description": "Identify most frequent adjacent symbol pair across vocabulary corpus and execute merge operation.\n\nBPE iteratively compresses common character sequences into single tokens, resolving out-of-vocabulary challenges while bounding total dictionary size.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n(s_1^*, s_2^*) = \\arg\\max_{(s_1, s_2)} \\sum_{w} \\text{freq}(w) \\cdot \\text{count}((s_1, s_2), w)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Count frequencies of all adjacent token pairs weighted by word frequencies\n2. Select pair with maximum occurrence count\n3. Merge occurrences of optimal pair in vocabulary words\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'best_pair', 'count', and updated 'merged_corpus'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Tokenizer Training\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Tokenizer Training** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nBPE iteratively compresses common character sequences into single tokens, resolving out-of-vocabulary challenges while bounding total dictionary size. In this problem, the goal is to: identify most frequent adjacent symbol pair across vocabulary corpus and execute merge operation.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n(s_1^*, s_2^*) = \\arg\\max_{(s_1, s_2)} \\sum_{w} \\text{freq}(w) \\cdot \\text{count}((s_1, s_2), w)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Count frequencies of all adjacent token pairs weighted by word frequencies\n2. **Phase 2**: Select pair with maximum occurrence count\n3. **Phase 3**: Merge occurrences of optimal pair in vocabulary words\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: corpus = [[\"l o w </w>\", 5], [\"l o w e r </w>\", 2], [\"n e w e s t </w>\", 6], [\"w i d e s t </w>\", 3]]\n\nOutput: {\"best_pair\": [\"e\", \"s\"], \"count\": 9, \"merged_corpus\": [[\"l o w </w>\", 5], [\"l o w e r </w>\", 2], [\"n e w es t </w>\", 6], [\"w i d es t </w>\", 3]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_tokenizer_training_bpe under standard test conditions."
