  "starterCode": "def nlp_tokenizer_training_bpe(corpus: list) -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Tokenizer Training** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\ndef nlp_tokenizer_training_bpe(corpus: list) -> dict:\n    counts = {}\n    for w, f in corpus:\n        t = w.split()\n        for i in range(len(t) - 1):\n            p = (t[i], t[i+1])\n            counts[p] = counts.get(p, 0) + f\n    bp = max(counts.keys(), key=lambda k: counts[k])\n    mp = ''.join(bp)\n    nc = []\n    for w, f in corpus:\n        t = w.split()\n        nt, i = [], 0\n        while i < len(t):\n            if i < len(t) - 1 and (t[i], t[i+1]) == bp:\n                nt.append(mp); i += 2\n            else:\n                nt.append(t[i]); i += 1\n        nc.append([' '.join(nt), f])\n    return {'best_pair': list(bp), 'count': counts[bp], 'merged_corpus': nc}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
